=====================================
GENESIS Regression Tests
=====================================

Regression tests are prepared for ATDYN, SPDYN, CGDYN, prst_setup
(parallel I/O), and analysis tools to check if these programs
work correctly.

In order to run the tests, execute a Python script
(regression_test/test.py) in the following way:

  $ cd regression_test/
  $ ./test.py [genesis command] [parallel_io (optional)]

Here, [genesis command] is a command line for executing genesis
simulators (ATDYN, SPDYN, or CGDYN). The default is "mpirun -np 8 atdyn".
[parallel_io] is appended when checking the parallel I/O function.
Examples are follows:

  # execute atdyn test
  $ ./test.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/atdyn"

  # execute spdyn test
  $ ./test.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn"

  # execute spdyn test on GPU environment
  $ ./test.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn" gpu

  # execute spdyn, parallel_io test
  $ ./test.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn" parallel_io

  # execute cgdyn test
  $ ./test.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/cgdyn"

  # execute REMD test
  $ ./test_remd.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/atdyn"
  $ ./test_remd.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn"

  # execute string method test
  $ ./test_rpath.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/atdyn"
  $ ./test_rpath.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn"

  # execute vibrational analysis test
  $ ./test_vib.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/atdyn"

  # execute GaMD test
  $ ./test_gamd.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/atdyn"
  $ ./test_gamd.py "mpirun -np 8 /path/to/spdyn"

The test of analysis tools is done in a similar way, but the
argument of the python script is the path to the bin directory.

  # test analysis tools
  $ cd test_analysis
  $ ./test_analysis.py /path/to/genesis/bin
  $ cd ..

  # test SPANA
  $ cd test_spana
  $ ./test_spana.py /path/to/genesis/bin
  $ cd ..

More detailed information about the regression test is described
in the user manual.

Electrostatic potential output (SPDYN)
====================================

After building double-precision SPDYN, run from the GENESIS root:

  $ python3 tests/regression_test/test_ele_solver.py --build .
  $ python3 tests/regression_test/test_ele_output.py --spdyn src/spdyn/spdyn
  $ python3 tests/regression_test/test_ele_existing.py --spdyn src/spdyn/spdyn

These checks require NumPy, mpifort, and mpirun. The integration test uses
up to eight MPI ranks and two OpenMP threads; provide a suitable mpirun
command with --mpiexec if the host needs additional launcher options.
All generated inputs and results are isolated in temporary directories.

The solver test links a small driver to the built analysis module and
compares with exact particle structure factors, independently of mesh
assignment. It checks normalization, complex B-spline phase, spline orders,
force-field Coulomb factors, nonneutral/zero-charge cells, dielectric
scaling, periodic translation, and fractional-source grid convergence.

The integration test covers the six standard FFT backends and automatic
selection, MPI/thread layouts, native odd-grid origins, field units and
signs, inactive requested fields, NPT box/field scaling, RESPA cadence,
invalid inputs, and output-on/off trajectory invariance. The numerical
tolerances in these two scripts target double precision. The CSV has only
five fixed comments (length/time/potential units, Ewald factor, dielectric);
tests recover frames and grids from the data rows and check them against
input parameters and engine logs. Build precision is recorded in the
simulation log, rather than the CSV.

To compare output-disabled behavior against an unchanged control binary:

  $ python3 tests/regression_test/test_ele_output.py \
      --spdyn src/spdyn/spdyn --baseline /path/to/control/spdyn

DCD comparisons exclude the human-readable title containing the creation
date; all remaining binary records and INFO energy lines must agree.
The existing-system check uses the DPPC VVER/NVT, DPPC VRES/NPT, and solid
nano-material VVER/NPT fixtures with applied fields, and accepts the same
optional --baseline argument.
